#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Intraposition/HealthPromotingIndex.md:8
msgid "HPI"
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/HealthPromotingIndex.md:16
msgid ""
"Represents the health-promoting index, which was proposed to assess the "
"nutritional value of dietary fat, with particular emphasis on the effects of "
"fatty acid composition on cardiovascular diseases (CVD). It is the exact "
"inverse of the index of atherogenicity (IA)."
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/HealthPromotingIndex.md:18
msgid ""
"From a human health perspective, the HPI value should be as high as "
"possible. This index evaluates the risk of cardiovascular diseases by "
"comparing fatty acids that inhibit plaque accumulation and lower levels of "
"phospholipids and cholesterol (MUFAs and PUFAs) against those that promote "
"lipid and plaque accumulation in blood vessels (such as lauric $C12:0$, "
"myristic $C14:0$, and palmitic $C16:0$ acids). Consuming foods with a higher "
"HPI value provides a very high degree of protection against atherogenic "
"diseases and is assumed to be more beneficial to human health."
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/HealthPromotingIndex.md:22
msgid ""
"[Chen _et al._, 2004](https://doi.org/10.1021/jf035193z \"Physical and "
"sensory properties of dairy products from cows with various milk fatty acid "
"compositions\")"
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/HypocholesterolemicToHypercholesterolemicIndex.md:12
msgid "HHI"
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/HypocholesterolemicToHypercholesterolemicIndex.md:16
msgid ""
"Represents the hypocholesterolemic/hypercholesterolemic index, which "
"evaluates the specific effect of fatty acids on cholesterol metabolism. It "
"calculates the ratio of hypocholesterolemic fatty acids (which help lower "
"blood cholesterol, such as oleic acid and polyunsaturated fatty acids) to "
"hypercholesterolemic fatty acids (which raise blood cholesterol, such as "
"lauric $C12:0$, myristic $C14:0$, and palmitic $C16:0$ acids)."
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/HypocholesterolemicToHypercholesterolemicIndex.md:18
msgid ""
"From a nutritional point of view, **high** HHI values are highly desirable. "
"Unlike the atherogenic (AI) and thrombogenic (TI) indices where lower values "
"are better, a higher HHI indicates a healthier lipid profile with a greater "
"proportion of beneficial fatty acids that contribute to the reduction of "
"plasma cholesterol levels. It is considered a highly suitable metric for "
"assessing the health benefits of lipids in food products and their "
"protective role against cardiovascular diseases."
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/NutritionalValueIndex.md:10
msgid "NVI"
msgstr "NVI"

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/NutritionalValueIndex.md:14
msgid ""
"Represents the nutritional value index, a metric developed to evaluate the "
"nutritional quality of lipids, particularly in foods of animal origin where "
"stearic, oleic, and palmitic fatty acids are dominant. It is calculated as "
"the ratio of the sum of stearic acid ($C18:0$) and oleic acid ($C18:1\\[\\"
"\\omega-9\\]$) to palmitic acid ($C16:0$)."
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/NutritionalValueIndex.md:16
msgid ""
"From a human health perspective, a higher NVI value is considered more "
"favorable. Palmitic acid ($C16:0$) is known to be atherogenic and is "
"associated with increased levels of LDL cholesterol. In contrast, stearic "
"acid ($C18:0$) is generally considered to have a neutral effect on blood "
"cholesterol metabolism, while oleic acid ($C18:1\\[\\\\omega-9\\]$), a "
"monounsaturated fatty acid, has beneficial hypocholesterolemic properties. "
"Therefore, a higher ratio of these neutral/beneficial fatty acids to the "
"atherogenic palmitic acid indicates a healthier lipid profile."
msgstr ""

#: src/FattyAcids/Ratio/Nutritional/NutritionalValueIndex.md:20
msgid ""
"[Chen _et al._, 2016](https://doi.org/10.5713/ajas.15.0840 \"Differences in "
"Physicochemical and Nutritional Properties of Breast and Thigh Meat from "
"Crossbred Chickens, Commercial Broilers, and Spent Hens\")"
